# Delivery modes

A *target* is where the nexus lives; a *delivery mode* is how a page reaches a
browser. The same workbook ships three ways with no change to the source — because
placement (`client` / `server`) is already on every unit, the mode just decides who
renders the server regions and how the diff travels.

server compute :delivery do
  defmodule Mode do
    defstruct name: :static, renders: :browser, transport: :file
  end

  # (a) Static bundle — no nexus. salesapp.html is a file you can email; server
  #     regions were rendered once at weave time, client islands hydrate in the browser.
  def static, do: %Mode{name: :static, renders: :weave_time, transport: :file}

  # (b) Nexus SSR — a target is live. Server regions ([[Pipeline]], the [[Plan]]
  #     board) render on the BEAM per request; client units arrive as islands.
  def nexus_ssr, do: %Mode{name: :nexus_ssr, renders: :beam, transport: :http}

  # (c) Live SSR — same server rendering, but the nexus streams diffs over the RCP
  #     websocket. The board mutates in place as agents claim tasks.
  def live_ssr, do: %Mode{name: :live_ssr, renders: :beam, transport: :rcp_ws}
end

| mode | server regions render | transport | needs a target |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `:static` | once, at weave time | a `.html` file | no |
| `:nexus_ssr` | on the BEAM, per request | HTTP | yes |
| `:live_ssr` | on the BEAM, per request | RCP websocket | yes |

One authoring model, every delivery mode. You learned the format by reading a real
app; now you ship it. Back to [[Welcome]] to read it with the whole picture in view.
